1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,515
Welcome to chapter seventeen of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,515 --> 00:00:10,483
This is the chapter where the project leaves your laptop.

3
00:00:10,483 --> 00:00:17,236
We deploy an LLM API to Modal, a serverless GPU platform, using a few decorators and one command.

4
00:00:17,236 --> 00:00:29,071
I'll walk through every step, check the prices against Modal's pricing page as of today, and point out two problems in the book's code that would trip you up on a T4 GPU.

5
00:00:29,921 --> 00:00:34,568
In chapter sixteen we built a production style FastAPI service.

6
00:00:34,568 --> 00:00:38,183
Today we put it on a cloud GPU with a public URL.

7
00:00:38,183 --> 00:00:42,756
The chapter seventeen notebook doesn't deploy anything itself.

8
00:00:42,756 --> 00:00:48,289
It prints the files you save and then run with the modal command line tool.

9
00:00:49,139 --> 00:00:49,872
The plan.

10
00:00:49,872 --> 00:00:51,665
What serverless means.

11
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:55,739
Three small Modal programs that build up to a GPU.

12
00:00:55,739 --> 00:00:56,879
Web endpoints.

13
00:00:56,879 --> 00:00:58,590
The full LLM service.

14
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:00,220
Secrets and volumes.

15
00:01:00,220 --> 00:01:03,805
Choosing a GPU, logs, costs and cold starts.

16
00:01:03,805 --> 00:01:04,538
Security.

17
00:01:04,538 --> 00:01:08,531
Then the fixes the book's code needs, and review.

18
00:01:09,381 --> 00:01:10,109
Key terms.

19
00:01:10,109 --> 00:01:14,839
Serverless means you ship code and the platform handles machines.

20
00:01:14,839 --> 00:01:19,860
A container packages the operating system, Python and your libraries.

21
00:01:19,860 --> 00:01:23,935
A cold start is the time to boot one and load the model.

22
00:01:23,935 --> 00:01:27,065
A volume is storage that survives restarts.

23
00:01:27,065 --> 00:01:29,030
A secret holds keys safely.

24
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:33,978
And the scale down window is how long an idle container stays alive.

25
00:01:34,828 --> 00:01:35,478
Part one.

26
00:01:35,478 --> 00:01:37,068
Why serverless at all?

27
00:01:37,918 --> 00:01:40,858
The book's analogy is a car versus a taxi.

28
00:01:40,858 --> 00:01:47,578
A traditional server is rented around the clock, you pay while it sits idle and you maintain it.

29
00:01:47,578 --> 00:01:53,598
Serverless runs only when a request arrives, bills per second and scales down to zero.

30
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:57,265
I checked Modal's pricing page today.

31
00:01:57,265 --> 00:02:06,858
A T4 is about fifty nine cents an hour, an A10 about a dollar ten and an H100 about three ninety five, which matches the book.

32
00:02:06,858 --> 00:02:11,426
The starter plan includes thirty dollars of compute a month.

33
00:02:11,426 --> 00:02:15,842
That's about fifty T4 hours if the GPU were the only cost.

34
00:02:15,842 --> 00:02:20,715
In practice CPU and memory are billed too, so expect a bit less.

35
00:02:20,715 --> 00:02:27,871
For a demo used a few hours a month, serverless is far cheaper than a server rented all month.

36
00:02:28,722 --> 00:02:30,398
Setup is three commands.

37
00:02:30,398 --> 00:02:38,640
Install the package, run modal setup, which opens a browser and stores a token on your machine, and check the version.

38
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:40,945
Treat that token like a password.

39
00:02:41,796 --> 00:02:42,398
Part two.

40
00:02:42,398 --> 00:02:44,206
Building up in small steps.

41
00:02:45,056 --> 00:02:46,617
The smallest Modal app.

42
00:02:46,617 --> 00:02:52,656
Create an app, decorate a function, and call it with dot remote from a local entry point.

43
00:02:52,656 --> 00:02:59,712
Modal run starts a container in the cloud, runs the function there and prints the result on your laptop.

44
00:03:00,562 --> 00:03:01,340
Four ideas.

45
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:04,026
The app is a named group of functions.

46
00:03:04,026 --> 00:03:07,561
The function decorator marks code to run remotely.

47
00:03:07,561 --> 00:03:13,004
Dot remote calls it in the cloud, and dot local would run it on your machine.

48
00:03:13,004 --> 00:03:19,719
Behind the scenes Modal built a container, ran your code, sent back the value and shut it down.

49
00:03:20,570 --> 00:03:22,968
Adding a GPU is one argument.

50
00:03:22,968 --> 00:03:32,809
The function imports torch inside its body, because torch only needs to exist inside the container, not on your laptop.

51
00:03:32,809 --> 00:03:35,290
On a T4 it reports a Tesla T4.

52
00:03:36,140 --> 00:03:40,824
The image says what to install, in Python instead of a Dockerfile.

53
00:03:40,824 --> 00:03:45,083
The first run builds and caches it, and later runs reuse it.

54
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:51,116
My tip: pin versions, so a rebuild next month doesn't quietly pull a different torch.

55
00:03:51,966 --> 00:03:52,913
Part three.

56
00:03:52,913 --> 00:03:54,291
Giving it a URL.

57
00:03:55,141 --> 00:04:01,926
Any FastAPI app can be served by returning it from a function decorated with asgi app.

58
00:04:01,926 --> 00:04:11,866
Modal deploy, not modal run, keeps it running and prints a public HTTPS URL built from your workspace, app and function names.

59
00:04:12,716 --> 00:04:16,272
Run is ephemeral and stops when your terminal does.

60
00:04:16,272 --> 00:04:19,271
Deploy is persistent and is what you share.

61
00:04:19,271 --> 00:04:26,732
There's also modal serve, which gives you a temporary URL that reloads as you edit, handy while developing.

62
00:04:27,582 --> 00:04:29,926
Here's the whole flow, redrawn.

63
00:04:29,926 --> 00:04:40,585
A Python file on your laptop, modal deploy, an image build and a container on a GPU, and a public URL with your health, chat and docs routes.

64
00:04:40,585 --> 00:04:46,633
The docs page is the same Swagger interface you used locally in chapter sixteen.

65
00:04:47,483 --> 00:04:48,347
Part four.

66
00:04:48,347 --> 00:04:50,768
A real model behind the URL.

67
00:04:51,618 --> 00:04:56,709
The service installs FastAPI and vLLM, a fast inference engine.

68
00:04:56,709 --> 00:05:07,538
A global holds the model, and get model loads Qwen two point five, one point five billion, the first time it's called, then reuses it.

69
00:05:07,538 --> 00:05:09,154
That's lazy loading.

70
00:05:10,004 --> 00:05:21,906
The chat endpoint takes a validated request, generates with vLLM and returns a validated response, the same Pydantic pattern as chapter sixteen.

71
00:05:21,906 --> 00:05:25,130
Exceptions become an HTTP five hundred.

72
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:33,460
The decorators set the image, the GPU, a five minute timeout and a five minute scale down window.

73
00:05:33,460 --> 00:05:40,863
Concurrent with ten inputs lets one container, and one loaded model, serve ten requests at once.

74
00:05:40,863 --> 00:05:43,793
vLLM batches them together on the GPU.

75
00:05:44,643 --> 00:05:48,110
Here's the first fix the book's code needs.

76
00:05:48,110 --> 00:05:55,045
Qwen two point five is stored in bfloat sixteen, and a T4 doesn't support that format.

77
00:05:55,045 --> 00:06:04,076
vLLM version zero point six refuses to start with an error saying bfloat sixteen needs compute capability eight.

78
00:06:04,076 --> 00:06:10,688
There's a public vLLM issue showing exactly this with Qwen two point five on a T4.

79
00:06:10,688 --> 00:06:12,462
The fix is dtype half.

80
00:06:12,462 --> 00:06:18,187
I'd also cap the context length so the cache fits in sixteen gigabytes.

81
00:06:18,187 --> 00:06:22,461
Or choose an L4 or A10, which support bfloat sixteen.

82
00:06:23,311 --> 00:06:26,683
The second issue is quality rather than a crash.

83
00:06:26,683 --> 00:06:35,957
The service passes the raw message to an instruct model with no chat template, so it tends to ramble or just continue your sentence.

84
00:06:35,957 --> 00:06:39,891
vLLM's chat method applies the model's template for you.

85
00:06:39,891 --> 00:06:40,804
I'd use that.

86
00:06:41,654 --> 00:06:46,578
Test with curl: the health route, then a chat post with a JSON body.

87
00:06:46,578 --> 00:06:51,357
Expect the first call after the app has been idle to take a while.

88
00:06:51,357 --> 00:06:52,806
That's a cold start.

89
00:06:52,806 --> 00:06:54,326
Or use the docs page.

90
00:06:55,176 --> 00:06:55,972
Part five.

91
00:06:55,972 --> 00:06:57,245
Keys and caches.

92
00:06:58,096 --> 00:07:02,307
Create a secret once from the command line or the dashboard.

93
00:07:02,307 --> 00:07:07,009
Attach it to a function, and it appears as an environment variable.

94
00:07:07,009 --> 00:07:16,485
One detail: to protect the chat endpoint, attach the secret to the serve function itself, otherwise the API key check never sees a key.

95
00:07:17,335 --> 00:07:26,363
A volume mounted at the Hugging Face cache folder means the weights download once and every later cold start reads them from the volume.

96
00:07:26,363 --> 00:07:30,412
Mount it on the serve function too, not only a demo function.

97
00:07:31,262 --> 00:07:31,971
Part six.

98
00:07:31,971 --> 00:07:33,545
Running it sensibly.

99
00:07:34,395 --> 00:07:37,533
Here's my table from the current pricing page.

100
00:07:37,533 --> 00:07:40,536
The T4 is cheapest but lacks bfloat sixteen.

101
00:07:40,536 --> 00:07:47,427
An L4 costs a little more, supports it and is faster, which makes it a good default for small models.

102
00:07:47,427 --> 00:07:53,636
A rough memory rule: two bytes per parameter in half precision, plus room for the KV cache.

103
00:07:53,636 --> 00:07:58,480
A one and a half billion model needs about three gigabytes for weights.

104
00:07:59,330 --> 00:08:05,402
A cold start is boot time, plus downloading weights, plus loading them onto the GPU.

105
00:08:05,402 --> 00:08:08,077
This is my sketch, not a measurement.

106
00:08:08,077 --> 00:08:16,246
A volume removes the download, and a longer scale down window keeps a container warm between requests, at a cost.

107
00:08:16,246 --> 00:08:20,728
For a hobby project, an occasional slow first request is fine.

108
00:08:21,578 --> 00:08:28,929
Logs stream from the command line or the dashboard, and your chapter sixteen JSON logs appear there unchanged.

109
00:08:28,929 --> 00:08:31,135
Updating is just deploying again.

110
00:08:31,135 --> 00:08:34,276
For cost, let it scale to zero and start small.

111
00:08:34,276 --> 00:08:38,887
One correction: the book says you'll get an email before any charges.

112
00:08:38,887 --> 00:08:47,241
Modal's pricing page says usage beyond the thirty dollars is pay as you go, so set a workspace budget if you want a hard cap.

113
00:08:48,091 --> 00:08:49,046
Part seven.

114
00:08:49,046 --> 00:08:50,694
Your API is public.

115
00:08:51,544 --> 00:08:54,492
The book's API key check has a small trap.

116
00:08:54,492 --> 00:09:04,387
Header with no default makes the header required, so any request without it gets a four twenty two validation error, even when no key is set.

117
00:09:04,387 --> 00:09:07,124
I tested it with FastAPI's test client.

118
00:09:07,124 --> 00:09:12,387
Give it a default of None, and then the code behaves as the book describes.

119
00:09:13,237 --> 00:09:13,920
The rest.

120
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:15,363
HTTPS is automatic.

121
00:09:15,363 --> 00:09:18,323
Reuse your rate limiter and validation.

122
00:09:18,323 --> 00:09:24,777
Put an upper limit on max tokens, so a single request can't run your GPU for minutes.

123
00:09:24,777 --> 00:09:34,570
And remember that an in memory rate limiter counts per container, so if Modal scales to three containers, each has its own count.

124
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:37,193
Modal isn't the only route.

125
00:09:37,193 --> 00:09:44,549
The book's appendix covers Docker on a rented server, which gives full control but costs money around the clock.

126
00:09:44,549 --> 00:09:48,686
Hugging Face Spaces and inference endpoints are good for demos.

127
00:09:48,686 --> 00:09:53,940
And a local model plus a tunnel is free but only works while your machine is on.

128
00:09:53,940 --> 00:09:58,077
The nice thing is that your FastAPI app is the same everywhere.

129
00:09:58,077 --> 00:09:59,719
Only the wrapper changes.

130
00:10:00,569 --> 00:10:01,377
Part eight.

131
00:10:01,377 --> 00:10:01,891
Review.

132
00:10:02,742 --> 00:10:03,766
Short answers.

133
00:10:03,766 --> 00:10:09,181
Serverless bills per second and scales to zero, perfect for spiky traffic.

134
00:10:09,181 --> 00:10:10,791
A GPU is one argument.

135
00:10:10,791 --> 00:10:16,206
A cold start is boot plus model load, reduced by volumes and warm windows.

136
00:10:16,206 --> 00:10:19,133
Volumes keep weights between containers.

137
00:10:19,133 --> 00:10:23,670
And with no traffic, the app scales to zero and costs nothing.

138
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:26,635
My version of the checkpoint.

139
00:10:26,635 --> 00:10:29,554
Deploy hello world, then a GPU function.

140
00:10:29,554 --> 00:10:35,464
Deploy the service with half precision or on an L4, a volume and the chat method.

141
00:10:35,464 --> 00:10:36,997
Test from your phone.

142
00:10:36,997 --> 00:10:40,134
Add the API key secret with the header fix.

143
00:10:40,134 --> 00:10:43,272
And set a budget before you share the link.

144
00:10:44,122 --> 00:10:49,537
Practice: deploy the simple API and time a cold versus a warm request.

145
00:10:49,537 --> 00:10:54,256
Swap in your own chapter twelve model behind the same routes.

146
00:10:54,256 --> 00:10:58,820
And compare tokens per second per dollar on a T4 and an L4.

147
